# Summary
Introduced an information disclosure risk by logging full query strings and SigV4 credential fields during request authentication and request start tracing.
The commit adds request tracing that prints user-controlled request metadata (method/path/query) and SigV4 POST credentials directly to stderr. For presigned URLs, the query string contains X-Amz-Signature/X-Amz-Credential parameters that function as bearer tokens for the duration of the URL. Writing them to logs risks credential/token disclosure to anyone with log access or to log aggregation systems. This information leak is introduced by the new observability instrumentation when ARGMIN_TRACE is enabled.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm request_start tracing logs method/path/query (serve.rs:294-313)
- [x] Confirm authenticate_request trace logs raw query_string (auth/request.rs:66-73)
- [x] Confirm POST SigV4 trace logs credential field (auth/post.rs:24-30)
- [x] Confirm tracing is enabled by ARGMIN_TRACE and logs to stderr (observability/lib.rs:52-67, 179-188, 245-255)
- [x] Establish that presigned SigV4 uses query parameters that would be logged (auth/request.rs:104-110 checks for X-Amz-Algorithm in query string)
## Report
Crash/valgrind/debugger attempts: `cargo build -p argmin-s3` failed because `libisal` was missing (ec-sys build.rs: "libisal not found; install intel-isa-l or set PKG_CONFIG_PATH"), so no runtime reproduction possible. `valgrind` and `gdb` are not installed in the container. Code inspection confirms the info leak: server HTTP request handling logs the raw query string in `request_start` (crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:294-313). Authentication traces log raw query strings during request auth (crates/auth/src/request.rs:66-73) and log the SigV4 credential field during POST auth (crates/auth/src/post.rs:24-30). Tracing is enabled by `ARGMIN_TRACE` and emitted to stderr via `eprintln!` in the tracing infrastructure (crates/observability/src/lib.rs:52-67, 179-188, 245-255). Therefore, when tracing is enabled, full query strings (including presigned `X-Amz-*` parameters) and SigV4 credential identifiers are written to logs, matching the suspected information disclosure.

