# Attack-path analysis
Final: low | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
The code does log attacker-controlled query strings and credential fields when ARGMIN_TRACE is enabled, but this is opt-in and requires log access. The leaked data is transient (presigned tokens) or identifier-level (credential field), so overall impact and likelihood remain low.
## Likelihood
low - Requires tracing to be enabled and an attacker or unintended party to access logs, making exploitation less likely in typical deployments.
## Impact
low - Leakage is limited to logs; presigned URL signatures are short-lived and POST credential fields reveal identifiers rather than secrets. Exposure depends on log access.
## Assumptions
- ARGMIN_TRACE is enabled in the deployment environment
- Attackers can send HTTP requests to the argmin-s3 endpoint
- Logs (stderr or aggregated logs) are accessible to someone other than the requester
- Presigned URL or SigV4 POST flows are in use so sensitive query/form fields appear in requests
- ARGMIN_TRACE enabled
- HTTP request reaches server
- Log access by a different principal
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4
## Path evidence
- `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:294-312` - request_start trace logs method, path, and full query string
- `crates/auth/src/request.rs:66-73` - authenticate_request trace logs raw query_string
- `crates/auth/src/post.rs:24-30` - POST authentication trace logs the SigV4 credential field
- `crates/observability/src/lib.rs:50-68` - ARGMIN_TRACE environment variable enables tracing
- `crates/observability/src/lib.rs:245-255` - trace events are emitted via eprintln! to stderr
- `crates/auth/src/request.rs:104-111` - presigned query parameters (X-Amz-Algorithm) are parsed from query_string
## Narrative
ARGMIN_TRACE enables request tracing that prints request method/path/query and SigV4 POST credential fields to stderr. This includes attacker-controlled query strings and credential identifiers, which can contain presigned URL tokens (X-Amz-*) or POST credentials. Anyone with log access could recover these values while they remain valid. Tracing is opt-in and log access is an additional precondition, so impact and likelihood are limited.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication
- ARGMIN_TRACE opt-in logging
- Trace filter configuration (ARGMIN_TRACE_FILTER)
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests or logging pipeline configuration available to assess who can access stderr/log aggregation.
